ice Democratic chances at 96-97 percent", "Ken Buck's cross-party endorsement signals a weak Republican nominee"], "rationale": "The open seat favors Democrats on fundamentals: a D+6 state, a favorable national environment, and a Republican nominee struggling to consolidate his own side, as shown by former congressman Ken Buck's endorsement of Weiser. Expert ratings and betting markets align on a comfortable Democratic edge, and no polling evidence points the other way.", "watch": "A late shift in turnout dynamics or a Weiser scandal could narrow what markets and raters currently treat as a safe Democratic hold.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Open seat: Jared Polis (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Colorado's governorship is open for the first time in three terms, and Democrats enter as clear favorites to hold it. Attorney General Phil Weiser carries the party's standard against Republican Victor Marx, a lesser-known nominee whose campaign has drawn attention for campaign finance questions and an unconventional background. No public general-election polling tracks the race, so the outlook rests on state fundamentals, expert assessments, and betting markets.\n\nColorado has backed Democrats in statewide races consistently, and the midterm environment leans Democratic this cycle. Every major rating — from Cook, Inside Elections, NBC, Fox, and others — places the contest as Solid or Safe Democratic, and three prediction markets price Democratic chances at 96 to 97 percent. Former Republican congressman Ken Buck's endorsement of Weiser underscores the GOP nominee's difficulty consolidating his own party. A Democratic margin in the low double digits is the central expectation.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9695, "margin": 12.28}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9628, "margin": 12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9677, "margin": 12.5}, {"date": "